Identify the critical bug in this retry mechanism. Explain how it causes a stack overflow in CI but not locally.

```java
public class RetryAnalyzer implements IRetryAnalyzer {
    private int retryCount = 0;
    private static final int MAX_RETRY = 3;
    private static final Map<String, Boolean> flakyTestCache = new HashMap<>();

    @Override
    public boolean retry(ITestResult result) {
        if (!result.isSuccess() && retryCount < MAX_RETRY) {
            retryCount++;
            flakyTestCache.put(result.getName(), true);
            return true;
        }
        if (result.isSuccess() && retryCount > 0) {
            flakyTestCache.put(result.getName(), true);
        }
        return false;
    }

    public static boolean isFlaky(String testName) {
        return flakyTestCache.getOrDefault(testName, false);
    }

    public static void generateFlakyReport() {
        System.out.println("Flaky tests detected: " + flakyTestCache.size());
        flakyTestCache.forEach((test, val) -> System.out.println(test));
    }
}
```

TestNG calls retry() once per test invocation. When a test has multiple @Test methods and one fails, tests start throwing StackOverflowError after 2-3 retry cycles.

answer:
Bug 1 -- THE CRITICAL STACK OVERFLOW: RetryAnalyzer is instantiated ONCE per class but retryCount field is NOT reset between tests. Test A fails: retryCount goes 1, 2, 3. Test B fails: retryCount is already 3 (>= MAX_RETRY), so Test B gets NO retries. But the actual StackOverflowError: if the framework re-invokes the test method recursively instead of looping, each retry() pushes another frame on the call stack. After 3 retries with 5 tests, the stack accumulates 15 test frames + listener frames. In CI with -Xss512k (common in containers), the default 1024KB stack is exceeded, causing StackOverflowError.

Bug 2: flakyTestCache is a HashMap (NOT thread-safe). Under parallel execution, concurrent put() calls cause infinite loops in HashMap linked list traversal (Java 7) or data corruption (Java 8+ tree-bin operations).

Bug 3: The flaky detection produces false positives. If a test fails with a legitimate bug and retries 3 times (all failing), isFlaky = true even though the test is consistently failing with a real bug, not flaky.

Bug 4: generateFlakyReport() uses forEach on a HashMap that may be concurrently modified, throwing ConcurrentModificationException.

Fix:
```java
public class RetryAnalyzer implements IRetryAnalyzer {
    private ThreadLocal<Integer> retryCount = ThreadLocal.withInitial(() -> 0);
    private static final int MAX_RETRY = 3;
    private static final ConcurrentHashMap<String, Boolean> flakyCache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    @Override
    public boolean retry(ITestResult result) {
        int count = retryCount.get();
        if (!result.isSuccess() && count < MAX_RETRY) {
            retryCount.set(count + 1);
            return true;
        }
        if (result.isSuccess() && count > 0) flakyCache.put(result.getName(), true);
        retryCount.remove();
        return false;
    }
}
```

